Message-ID: <KFdGQr9xw-k.7ODwrxIYAgAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/KFdGQr9xw-k/m/7ODwrxIYAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi list,

I’m working with the package dsm in R and I have a problem choosing the best response distribution family for my DSM.

When I use quasipoisson family, the R-squared is good but the diagnostic plots are very bad and when I use negative binomial family, the R-squared is negative, but diagnostic plots are much better.

I have read that negative R-squared values are statistical artifacts and that they can be reported and interpreted as being 0… Then, why are negative binomial diagnostic plots better than quasipoisson plots?

Which could be the problem or the explanation for this?

Which family should I choose?

Thanks,

Paula.

